## an8.53
an8.53:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: I have heard that at on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesāli at the Gabled Hall in the Great Forest.
an8.53:1.2 | S: Then Mahāpajāpati Gotamī went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Mahāpajāpati Gotamī went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, stood to one side. As she was standing there she said to him:
an8.53:2.1 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “It would be good, lord, if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief such that, having heard the Dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute.”
an8.53:2.2 | S: “Gotamī, you might know that certain things | T: “Gotamī, the qualities of which you may know,
an8.53:2.3 | S: lead to passion, not dispassion; | T: ‘These qualities lead to passion, not to dispassion;
an8.53:2.4 | S: to yoking, not to unyoking; | T: to being fettered, not to being unfettered;
an8.53:2.5 | S: to accumulation, not dispersal; | T: to accumulating, not to shedding;
an8.53:2.6 | S: to more desires, not fewer; | T: to self-aggrandizement, not to modesty;
an8.53:2.7 | S: to lack of contentment, not contentment; | T: to discontent, not to contentment;
an8.53:2.8 | S: to crowding, not seclusion; | T: to entanglement, not to reclusiveness;
an8.53:2.9 | S: to laziness, not energy; | T: to laziness, not to aroused persistence;
an8.53:2.10 | S: to being burdensome, not being unburdensome. | T: to being burdensome, not to being unburdensome’:
an8.53:2.11 | S: Categorically, you should remember these things as | T: You may categorically hold,
an8.53:2.12 | S: not the teaching, not the training, and not the Teacher’s instructions. | T: ‘This is not the Dhamma, this is not the Vinaya, this is not the Teacher’s instruction.’
an8.53:3.1 | S: You might know that certain things | T: “As for the qualities of which you may know,
an8.53:3.2 | S: lead to dispassion, not passion; | T: ‘These qualities lead to dispassion, not to passion;
an8.53:3.3 | S: to unyoking, not to yoking; | T: to being unfettered, not to being fettered;
an8.53:3.4 | S: to dispersal, not accumulation; | T: to shedding, not to accumulating;
an8.53:3.5 | S: to fewer desires, not more; | T: to modesty, not to self-aggrandizement;
an8.53:3.6 | S: to contentment, not lack of contentment; | T: to contentment, not to discontent;
an8.53:3.7 | S: to seclusion, not crowding; | T: to reclusiveness, not to entanglement;
an8.53:3.8 | S: to energy, not laziness; | T: to aroused persistence, not to laziness;
an8.53:3.9 | S: to being unburdensome, not being burdensome. | T: to being unburdensome, not to being burdensome’:
an8.53:3.10 | S: Categorically, you should remember these things as | T: You may categorically hold,
an8.53:3.11 | S: the teaching, the training, and the Teacher’s instructions.” | T: ‘This is the Dhamma, this is the Vinaya, this is the Teacher’s instruction.’” That is what the Blessed One said. Gratified, Mahāpājapati Gotamī delighted in his words.
an8.53:3.12 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.63
an8.63:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then a certain monk went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an8.63:1.2 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “It would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief so that, having heard the Dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone in seclusion: heedful, ardent, & resolute.”
an8.63:1.3 | S: “This is exactly how some futile people ask me for something. | T: “But it is in just this way that some worthless men make a request
an8.63:1.4 | S: But when the teaching has been explained they think only of following me around.” | T: but then, having been told the Dhamma, think they should tag along right behind me.”
an8.63:1.5 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief! May the Holy One teach me the Dhamma in brief! Hopefully I can understand the meaning of what the Buddha says! Hopefully I can be an heir of the Buddha’s teaching!” | T: “May the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma in brief! May the One Well-Gone teach me the Dhamma in brief! It may well be that I will understand the Blessed One’s words. It may well be that I will become an heir to the Blessed One’s words.”
an8.63:1.6 | S: “Well then, mendicant, you should train like this: | T: “Then, monk, you should train yourself thus:
an8.63:1.7 | S: ‘My mind will be steady and well settled internally. And bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen will not occupy my mind.’ | T: ‘My mind will be established inwardly, well-composed. No evil, unskillful qualities, once they have arisen, will remain consuming the mind.’
an8.63:1.8 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourself.
an8.63:2.1 | S: When your mind is steady and well settled internally, and bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen don’t occupy your mind, then you should train like this: | T: “Then you should train yourself thus:
an8.63:2.2 | S: ‘I will develop the heart’s release by love. I’ll cultivate it, make it my vehicle and my basis, keep it up, consolidate it, and properly implement it.’ | T: ‘Good-will, as my awareness-release, will be developed, pursued, given a means of transport, given a grounding, steadied, consolidated, & well-undertaken.’
an8.63:2.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourself.
an8.63:3.1 | S: When this immersion is developed and cultivated in this way, you should develop it while placing the mind and keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind, merely keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind or keeping it connected. You should develop it with rapture. You should develop it without rapture. You should develop it with pleasure. You should develop it with equanimity. | T: When you have developed this concentration in this way, you should develop this concentration with directed thought & evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & a modicum of evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & no evaluation, you should develop it accompanied by rapture… not accompanied by rapture… endowed with a sense of enjoyment; you should develop it endowed with equanimity.
an8.63:4.1 | S: When this immersion is well developed in this way, you should train like this: | T: “When this concentration is thus developed, thus well-developed by you, you should then train yourself thus:
an8.63:4.2 | S: ‘I will develop the heart’s release by compassion …’ … | T: ‘Compassion, as my awareness-release.…
an8.63:4.3 | S: ‘I will develop the heart’s release by rejoicing …’ … | T: Empathetic joy, as my awareness-release.…
an8.63:4.4 | S: ‘I will develop the heart’s release by equanimity. I’ll cultivate it, make it my vehicle and my basis, keep it up, consolidate it, and properly implement it.’ | T: Equanimity, as my awareness-release, will be developed, pursued, given a means of transport, given a grounding, steadied, consolidated, & well-undertaken.’
an8.63:4.5 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourself.
an8.63:5.1 | S: When this immersion is well developed in this way, you should develop it while placing the mind and keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind, merely keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind or keeping it connected. You should develop it with rapture. You should develop it without rapture. You should develop it with pleasure. You should develop it with equanimity. | T: When you have developed this concentration in this way, you should develop this concentration with directed thought & evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & a modicum of evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & no evaluation, you should develop it accompanied by rapture… not accompanied by rapture… endowed with a sense of enjoyment; you should develop it endowed with equanimity.
an8.63:6.1 | S: When this immersion is well developed in this way, you should train like this: | T: “When this concentration is thus developed, thus well-developed by you, you should then train yourself thus:
an8.63:6.2 | S: ‘I’ll meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world.’ | T: ‘I will remain focused on the body in & of itself—ardent, alert, & mindful—putting aside greed & distress with reference to the world.’
an8.63:6.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourself.
an8.63:7.1 | S: When this immersion is developed and cultivated in this way, you should develop it while placing the mind and keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind, merely keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind or keeping it connected. You should develop it with rapture. You should develop it without rapture. You should develop it with pleasure. You should develop it with equanimity. | T: When you have developed this concentration in this way, you should develop this concentration with directed thought & evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & a modicum of evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & no evaluation, you should develop it accompanied by rapture… not accompanied by rapture… endowed with a sense of enjoyment; you should develop it endowed with equanimity.
an8.63:8.1 | S: When this immersion is well developed in this way, you should train like this: | T: “When this concentration is thus developed, thus well-developed by you, you should train yourself:
an8.63:8.2 | S: ‘I’ll meditate on an aspect of feelings …’ … | T: ‘I will remain focused on feelings in & of themselves.…
an8.63:8.3 | S: ‘I’ll meditate on an aspect of the mind …’ … | T: the mind in & of itself…
an8.63:8.4 | S: ‘I’ll meditate on an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world.’ | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—putting aside greed & distress with reference to the world.’
an8.63:8.5 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourself.
an8.63:9.1 | S: When this immersion is developed and cultivated in this way, you should develop it while placing the mind and keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind, merely keeping it connected. You should develop it without placing the mind or keeping it connected. You should develop it with rapture. You should develop it without rapture. You should develop it with pleasure. You should develop it with equanimity. | T: When you have developed this concentration in this way, you should develop this concentration with directed thought & evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & a modicum of evaluation, you should develop it with no directed thought & no evaluation, you should develop it accompanied by rapture… not accompanied by rapture… endowed with a sense of enjoyment; you should develop it endowed with equanimity.
an8.63:10.1 | S: When this immersion is well developed in this way, wherever you walk, you’ll walk comfortably. Wherever you stand, you’ll stand comfortably. Wherever you sit, you’ll sit comfortably. Wherever you lie down, you’ll lie down comfortably.” | T: “When this concentration is thus developed, thus well-developed by you, then wherever you go, you will go in comfort. Wherever you stand, you will stand in comfort. Wherever you sit, you will sit in comfort. Wherever you lie down, you will lie down in comfort.”
an8.63:11.1 | S: When that mendicant had been given this advice by the Buddha, he rose from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: Then that monk, having been admonished by the admonishment from the Blessed One, got up from his seat and bowed down to the Blessed One, circled around him, keeping the Blessed One to his right side, and left.
an8.63:11.2 | S: Then that mendicant, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Then, dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute, he in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
an8.63:11.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
an8.63:11.4 | S: And that mendicant became one of the perfected. | T: And thus he became another one of the arahants.
an8.63:11.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.64
an8.64:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Gayā on Gayā Head. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Gayā at Gayā Head.
an8.64:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks, “Monks!” “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said,
an8.64:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, before my awakening—when I was still not awake but intent on awakening—I perceived light but did not see forms. | T: “Monks, before my self-awakening, when I was still just an unawakened bodhisatta, I perceived light but did not see forms.
an8.64:2.1 | S: Then it occurred to me, | T: The thought occurred to me,
an8.64:2.2 | S: ‘What if I were to both perceive light and see forms? | T: ‘If I perceived light and saw forms,
an8.64:2.3 | S: Then my knowledge and vision would become even more purified.’ | T: then this knowledge-&-vision of mine would be purer.’
an8.64:3.1 | S: So after some time, living diligent, keen, and resolute, I perceived light and saw forms. | T: “So, at a later time—staying heedful, ardent, & resolute—I perceived light and saw forms,
an8.64:3.2 | S: But I didn’t associate with those deities, converse, or engage in discussion. | T: but I did not associate with those devas, didn’t converse with them, or engage them in discussion.
