an8.64:10.1 | S: As long as my knowledge and vision about the deities was not fully purified in these eight rounds, I didn’t announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: “And, monks, as long as this—my eight-round heightened deva-knowledge-&-vision—was not pure, I did not claim to have directly awakened to the right self-awakening unexcelled in the cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, with its people with their contemplatives & brahmans, their royalty & commonfolk.
an8.64:10.2 | S: But when my knowledge and vision about the deities was fully purified in these eight rounds, I announced my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: But as soon as this—my eight-round heightened deva-knowledge-&-vision—was truly pure, then I did claim to have directly awakened to the right self-awakening unexcelled in the cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, with its people with their contemplatives & brahmans, their royalty & commonfolk.
an8.64:10.3 | S: Knowledge and vision arose in me: | T: Knowledge & vision arose in me:
an8.64:10.4 | S: ‘My freedom is unshakable; this is my last rebirth; now there’ll be no more future lives.’” | T: ‘Unprovoked is my release. This is the last birth. There is now no further becoming.’”
an8.64:10.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.6
an8.6:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around the eight worldly conditions. | T: “Monks, these eight worldly conditions spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions.
an8.6:1.2 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an8.6:1.3 | S: Gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, pleasure and pain. | T: Gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.
an8.6:1.4 | S: These eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around these eight worldly conditions. | T: These are the eight worldly conditions that spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions.
an8.6:2.1 | S: An unlearned ordinary person encounters gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, and pleasure and pain. | T: “For an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person there arise gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.
an8.6:2.2 | S: And so does a learned noble disciple. | T: For a well-instructed disciple of the noble ones there also arise gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.
an8.6:2.3 | S: What, then, is the difference between a learned noble disciple and an ordinary unlearned person?” | T: So what difference, what distinction, what distinguishing factor is there between the well-instructed disciple of the noble ones and the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person?”
an8.6:2.4 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “For us, lord, the teachings have the Blessed One as their root, their guide, & their arbitrator. It would be good if the Blessed One himself would explicate the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the monks will remember it.”
an8.6:3.1 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, monks, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an8.6:3.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an8.6:3.3 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an8.6:3.4 | S: “Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person encounters gain. | T: “Gain arises for an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person.
an8.6:3.5 | S: They don’t reflect: | T: He does not reflect,
an8.6:3.6 | S: ‘I’ve encountered this gain. | T: ‘Gain has arisen for me.
an8.6:3.7 | S: It’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable.’ They don’t truly understand it. | T: It is inconstant, stressful, & subject to change.’ He does not discern it as it has come to be.
an8.6:3.8 | S: They encounter loss … | T: “Loss arises.…
an8.6:3.9 | S: fame … | T: Status arises.…
an8.6:3.10 | S: disgrace … | T: Disgrace arises.…
an8.6:3.11 | S: blame … | T: Censure arises.…
an8.6:3.12 | S: praise … | T: Praise arises.…
an8.6:3.13 | S: pleasure … | T: Pleasure arises.…
an8.6:3.14 | S: pain. | T: “Pain arises.
an8.6:3.15 | S: They don’t reflect: | T: He does not reflect,
an8.6:3.16 | S: ‘I’ve encountered this pain. | T: ‘Pain has arisen for me.
an8.6:3.17 | S: It’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable.’ They don’t truly understand it. | T: It is inconstant, stressful, & subject to change.’ He does not discern it as it has come to be.
an8.6:4.1 | S: So gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, and pleasure and pain occupy their mind. | T: “His mind remains consumed with the gain. His mind remains consumed with the loss… with the status… the disgrace… the censure… the praise… the pleasure. His mind remains consumed with the pain.
an8.6:4.2 | S: They favor gain and oppose loss. | T: “He welcomes the arisen gain and rebels against the arisen loss.
an8.6:4.3 | S: They favor fame and oppose disgrace. | T: He welcomes the arisen status and rebels against the arisen disgrace.
an8.6:4.4 | S: They favor praise and oppose blame. | T: He welcomes the arisen praise and rebels against the arisen censure.
an8.6:4.5 | S: They favor pleasure and oppose pain. | T: He welcomes the arisen pleasure and rebels against the arisen pain.
an8.6:4.6 | S: Being so full of favoring and opposing, they’re not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: As he is thus engaged in welcoming & rebelling, he is not released from birth, aging, or death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, or despairs.
an8.6:4.7 | S: They’re not freed from suffering, I say. | T: He is not released, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an8.6:5.1 | S: A learned noble disciple encounters gain. | T: “Now, gain arises for a well-instructed disciple of the noble ones.
an8.6:5.2 | S: They reflect: | T: He reflects,
an8.6:5.3 | S: ‘I’ve encountered this gain. | T: ‘Gain has arisen for me.
an8.6:5.4 | S: It’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable.’ They truly understand it. | T: It is inconstant, stressful, & subject to change.’ He discerns it as it actually is.
an8.6:5.5 | S: They encounter loss … | T: “Loss arises.…
an8.6:5.6 | S: fame … | T: Status arises.…
an8.6:5.7 | S: disgrace … | T: Disgrace arises.…
an8.6:5.8 | S: blame … | T: Censure arises.…
an8.6:5.9 | S: praise … | T: Praise arises.…
an8.6:5.10 | S: pleasure … | T: Pleasure arises.…
an8.6:5.11 | S: pain. | T: “Pain arises.
an8.6:5.12 | S: They reflect: | T: He reflects,
an8.6:5.13 | S: ‘I’ve encountered this pain. | T: ‘Pain has arisen for me.
an8.6:5.14 | S: It’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable.’ They truly understand it. | T: It is inconstant, stressful, & subject to change.’ He discerns it as it actually is.
an8.6:6.1 | S: So gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, and pleasure and pain don’t occupy their mind. | T: “His mind does not remain consumed with the gain. His mind does not remain consumed with the loss… with the status… the disgrace… the censure… the praise… the pleasure. His mind does not remain consumed with the pain.
an8.6:6.2 | S: They don’t favor gain or oppose loss. | T: “He does not welcome the arisen gain, or rebel against the arisen loss.
an8.6:6.3 | S: They don’t favor fame or oppose disgrace. | T: He does not welcome the arisen status, or rebel against the arisen disgrace.
an8.6:6.4 | S: They don’t favor praise or oppose blame. | T: He does not welcome the arisen praise, or rebel against the arisen censure.
an8.6:6.5 | S: They don’t favor pleasure or oppose pain. | T: He does not welcome the arisen pleasure, or rebel against the arisen pain.
an8.6:6.6 | S: Having given up favoring and opposing, they’re freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: As he thus abandons welcoming & rebelling, he is released from birth, aging, & death; from sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs.
an8.6:6.7 | S: They’re freed from suffering, I say. | T: He is released, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an8.6:6.8 | S: This is the difference between a learned noble disciple and an unlearned ordinary person. | T: “This is the difference, this the distinction, this the distinguishing factor between the well-instructed disciple of the noble ones and the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person.”
an8.6:7.1 | S: Gain and loss, fame and disgrace, | T: Gain/loss, status/disgrace,
an8.6:7.2 | S: blame and praise, and pleasure and pain. | T: censure/praise, pleasure/pain:
an8.6:7.3 | S: These qualities among mankind are impermanent, | T: These conditions among human beings are inconstant,
an8.6:7.4 | S: transient, and perishable. | T: impermanent, subject to change.
an8.6:8.1 | S: An intelligent and mindful person knows these things, | T: Knowing this, mindful, the intelligent person,
an8.6:8.2 | S: seeing that they’re perishable. | T: ponders these changing conditions.
an8.6:8.3 | S: Desirable things don’t disturb their mind, | T: Desirable things don’t charm the mind,
an8.6:8.4 | S: nor are they repelled by the undesirable. | T: undesirable ones bring no resistance.
an8.6:9.1 | S: Both favoring and opposing | T: His welcoming & rebelling
an8.6:9.2 | S: are cleared and disappeared, they are no more. | T: are scattered, gone to their end, do not exist.
an8.6:9.3 | S: Knowing the stainless, sorrowless state, | T: Knowing the dustless, sorrowless state,
an8.6:9.4 | S: they who have gone beyond rebirth <j>understand rightly.” | T: he discerns rightly, has gone, beyond becoming, to the Further Shore.
an8.6:9.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.7
an8.7:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain, not long after Devadatta had left. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain, not long after Devadatta’s departure.
an8.7:1.2 | S: There the Buddha spoke to the mendicants about Devadatta: | T: There, referring to Devadatta, he addressed the monks:
an8.7:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, it’s good for a mendicant to check their own failings from time to time. | T: “Monks, it’s good for a monk periodically to have reflected on his own failings.
an8.7:1.4 | S: It’s good for a mendicant to check the failings of others from time to time. | T: It’s good for a monk periodically to have reflected on the failings of others.
